# Target C — exact train-cut coordinates reconstruct every core displacement

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening of the exact pure-tooth Section-126 normal form; theorem authority unchanged.  
**Dependencies:** proof-only Section 126 and the audited exact one-train shear primitive.

In the exact pure-tooth endpoint, the stopped trains do more than coordinatize the
contact infinitesimal-flex space. Their oriented cut vectors reconstruct the **actual
finite displacement between every two positive-core vertices**.

Choose, for each of the `q` outer-to-outer stopped trains `tau`, one of its exact
one-train shears. Let `u_tau` be the unit contact vector oriented so that the moving
shore has infinitesimal translation `J u_tau`. For core vertices `x,y`, define

\[
\epsilon_{xy}(\tau)=
\begin{cases}
+1,&x\text{ is on the moving shore and }y\text{ is on the fixed shore},\\
-1,&y\text{ is on the moving shore and }x\text{ is on the fixed shore},\\
0,&x,y\text{ lie on the same shore}.
\end{cases}
\tag{1}
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{
 p_x-p_y
 =\sum_{\tau=1}^q\epsilon_{xy}(\tau)u_\tau.
}
\tag{2}
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
|p_x-p_y|
\le
\#\{\tau:\tau\text{ separates }x,y\}.
}
\tag{3}
\]

Every actual diameter pair is therefore separated by at least `ceil(D)` stopped
trains. Since the pure-tooth endpoint has exactly `h=q` diameter edges,

\[
\boxed{
I_{H,T}:=
\#\{(h,\tau):h\text{ crosses the cut of }\tau\}
\ge q\lceil D\rceil.
}
\tag{4}
\]

Thus the Section-126 diameter/train derivative matrix is necessarily macroscopically
dense. Sparse support regressions remain useful only as warnings against purely local
matrix arguments; they are not metric models of a large pure-tooth survivor.

---

## 1. Sum of the exact train shears is global rotation modulo translation

Proof-only Section 126 gives the linear isomorphism

\[
\Phi:\mathcal F_C/\mathrm{Trans}\longrightarrow\mathbb R^q
\]

that records the angular rate on every stopped train. The exact one-train shear
`v^(tau)` has

\[
\Phi(v^{(\tau)})=e_\tau,
\]

the corresponding standard basis vector.

Therefore

\[
V:=\sum_{\tau=1}^qv^{(\tau)}
\]

has train-angle vector

\[
\Phi(V)=\mathbf1.
\tag{1.1}
\]

The infinitesimal Euclidean rotation

\[
R_x:=Jp_x
\]

also has angular rate one on every contact edge, hence

\[
\Phi(R)=\mathbf1.
\]

The kernel of `Phi` is exactly the translation space. Thus there is one constant vector
`t` such that on every positive-core vertex

\[
\boxed{
V_x=Jp_x+t.
}
\tag{1.2}
\]

Subtracting the equations for two vertices removes `t`:

\[
\boxed{
J(p_x-p_y)
=\sum_\tau(v_x^{(\tau)}-v_y^{(\tau)}).
}
\tag{1.3}

---

## 2. One shear contributes exactly one signed unit cut vector

For the chosen orientation of train `tau`, the exact shear fixes one Jordan shore and
translates every point of the other by

\[
R_\theta u_\tau-u_\tau.
\]

At `theta=0` the moving-shore velocity is

\[
Ju_\tau,
\]

while the fixed-shore velocity is zero. Hence

\[
\boxed{
 v_x^{(\tau)}-v_y^{(\tau)}
 =\epsilon_{xy}(\tau)Ju_\tau.
}
\tag{2.1}

Insert (2.1) into (1.3) and apply `-J`. This proves the reconstruction identity (2).

The statement is independent of the arbitrary choice of moving shore: reversing the
shear changes both `epsilon` and `u_tau` by the corresponding sign convention and
leaves their product unchanged.

---

## 3. Diameter incidence and vector balance

Let `h=xy` be an actual diameter edge and write

\[
r_h=p_x-p_y,
\qquad |r_h|=D.
\]

Equation (2) becomes

\[
\boxed{
 r_h=\sum_{\tau\pitchfork h}\epsilon_{h\tau}u_\tau,
}
\tag{3.1}
\]

where the sum is exactly over trains whose cuts separate the two diameter endpoints.
Taking norms gives

\[
D
\le
\sum_{\tau\pitchfork h}|u_\tau|
=\deg_T(h),
\]

so

\[
\boxed{\deg_T(h)\ge\lceil D\rceil.}
\tag{3.2}
\]

In the pure-tooth endpoint `h=q`; summing (3.2) over all diameter rows proves (4).
Equivalently, the average diameter incidence of a stopped train is at least
`ceil(D)`.

The vector identity also splits into exact longitudinal and transverse balances. If
`n_h=r_h/D`, then

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{\tau\pitchfork h}
\epsilon_{h\tau}(n_h\cdot u_\tau)=D,
}
\tag{3.3}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{\tau\pitchfork h}
\epsilon_{h\tau}(n_h\cdot Ju_\tau)=0.
}
\tag{3.4}
\]

The second identity is exactly the row-sum-zero relation in the Section-126 diameter
derivative matrix, now seen as one coordinate of the stronger vector reconstruction.

---

## 4. Exact local tooth check

For consecutive loaded hull vertices `b_i,b_{i+1}` in the pure-tooth endpoint, the
outer contact route is exactly

\[
b_i-x_i-b_{i+1}
\]

with two unit contact edges. These edges belong to two distinct train cuts. The path
crosses exactly those two cuts with nonzero signed crossing number, so (2) specializes
to the elementary tooth identity

\[
p_{b_{i+1}}-p_{b_i}
=u_{\tau_1}+u_{\tau_2}
\]

after orienting the two unit contact vectors along the route. Thus the global
reconstruction is consistent with every local two-edge tooth.

---

## 5. Research consequence

Any future pure-tooth matrix regression must now satisfy a macroscopic support-density
condition absent from the support-only family:

\[
\min_{h\in H}\deg_T(h)\ge D,
\qquad
{I_{H,T}\over q^2}\ge {D\over q}+o(1).
\]

More importantly, the nonzero coefficients cannot be chosen independently on that
support: for every diameter row the signed unit train directions themselves must sum
to the actual diameter vector as in (3.1).

This is the next viable closure currency. A successful theorem could combine

- the convex-thrackle cyclic order of diameter chords;
- the triangle-free crossing order of train chords;
- the exact vector sums (3.1); and
- the near-disk loaded-hull geometry

to force either a forbidden train crossing pattern or a strict diameter descent.

No such final incompatibility is claimed here.

---

## 6. Scope audit

- The reconstruction concerns the nonisolated positive-contact core. Contact-isolated
  source points remain in the full source and are not placed in the Section-57 kernel.
- The proof uses the exact full-source train shear only to obtain its infinitesimal
  velocity on the core; no response theorem is invoked.
- `epsilon_{xy}(tau)` is a signed shore-separation number, not an intersection
  multiplicity of arbitrary curves.
- Every train vector has norm exactly one because it is an actual unit-contact vector.
- The incidence floor is finite and exact.

**Disposition:** proof-only structural strengthening. C-II remains open.